<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suriname</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suriname is a country on the north-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, formerly a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colony. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> note that Suriname became independent on 25 November 1975. This closed the Dutch colonial account.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suriname.jpg" alt="Suriname" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Suriname.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Suriname lies on the Guiana Shield on the northern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> to the north, French Guiana to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a> to the west. It is the smallest sovereign country in South America. The northern lowland coast has been cultivated, and most of the people live there. The south is tropical rainforest and sparsely inhabited savanna, covering about 80% of the land. The name may come from an Indigenous people called the Surinen, who lived there at the time of European contact. British settlers wrote it Surinam, which long remained the standard English spelling until the official change to Suriname in January 1978.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Indigenous settlement dates back to about 3,000 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lokono">Lokono</a> were a nomadic coastal people who lived by hunting, foraging and fishing, and the Carib, using superior sailing ships, conquered them. British settlers founded the first European colony at Marshall's Creek on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname-river">Suriname River</a> in 1630. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal, began to settle from the early 17th century and were granted freedom of religion in 1665. In 1667, in the negotiations leading to the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breda">Breda</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> kept the plantation colony they had gained from the English, and the English kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>, which they renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule Suriname was a lucrative plantation colony, mostly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, driven by African slave labour. About 300,000 enslaved Africans were taken there from the mid-1600s to the early 1800s. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished in 1863, indentured servants were recruited mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. Suriname became a constituent country of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a> in 1954 and independent on 25 November 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Suriname is a representative democratic republic under the Constitution of 1987. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paramaribo">Paramaribo</a> is the capital and largest city, home to nearly half the population. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> is the official language, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sranan-tongo">Sranan Tongo</a>, an English-based creole, is widely used. No ethnic group forms a majority. The population includes descendants of indentured workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and of enslaved Africans, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a>, whose ancestors escaped to the interior. The Central Suriname Nature Reserve, established in 1998, became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suriname” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Suriname</b>'s independence on 25 November 1975 ended the Dutch colonial empire, leaving only the Caribbean islands as self-governing parts of the Kingdom of the Netherlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Overseas, the VOC’s posts at Batavia, in Ceylon and at the Cape and the West India Company’s colonies in New Netherland, <b>Suriname</b> and the Caribbean left lasting marks, including New York, founded as New Amsterdam, and the Afrikaans-speaking population of South Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Charter for the Kingdom of 1954 made the Netherlands, <b>Suriname</b> and the Netherlands Antilles equal partners in one kingdom, and since 2010 the Kingdom has consisted of the Netherlands, Aruba, Curaçao and Sint Maarten.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Breda of 1667 ended the Second Anglo-Dutch War; the Dutch did not press their claim, keeping <b>Suriname</b> and the nutmeg island of Run instead.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Treaty of Breda of 1667 the Dutch kept <b>Suriname</b> while the English kept New Amsterdam, which they had renamed New York.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Keti Koti, on 1 July, marks the abolition of 1863 in <b>Suriname</b> and the Dutch Caribbean, and Juneteenth, on 19 June, recalls the announcement of freedom in Galveston, Texas, in 1865 and became a federal holiday in the United States in 2021.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
